<section class="fragment-plan" data-artifact="plan" data-slice="stream-protocol" data-rev="0">
  <table class="files-touched pl-files">
    <thead><tr><th>File</th><th>Status</th><th>Module</th><th>Lines</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr class="is-modified"><td><details><summary><span class="pl-chip">config</span> Cargo.toml, crates/protocol/Cargo.toml, crates/stream/Cargo.toml, crates/engine-cli/Cargo.toml</summary><div class="pl-card">Two new workspace members; tungstenite 0.30 with the handshake feature and no transport-layer security, about eight permissive transitive crates. The protocol crate depends on serde and thiserror only, so it carries no network dependency.</div></details></td><td>modified</td><td>workspace root</td><td>+50 / -1</td></tr>
      <tr class="is-new"><td><details><summary><span class="pl-chip">domain</span> crates/protocol/src/lib.rs, message.rs, codec.rs, event.rs, command.rs, frame.rs</summary><div class="pl-card">PROTOCOL_VERSION 1; the six server messages and four client commands as serde enums with dotted keys; the tick codec, a 98-byte keyframe every 50 ticks and 47-byte delta frames of signed centimetre steps between, encoded into a reused buffer; the match-event record with kick-off, goal, and full-time; the change queue with its identifiers. No input or output anywhere in the crate.</div></details></td><td>new</td><td>crates/protocol</td><td>+870</td></tr>
      <tr class="is-new"><td><details><summary><span class="pl-chip">service</span> crates/stream/src/lib.rs, server.rs, session.rs, control.rs, record.rs, replay.rs, events.rs</summary><div class="pl-card">The blocking WebSocket server on 127.0.0.1:0 with an Origin allowlist and a version check, one thread per client; the socket sink and its bounded channel of 500 ticks, whose full state blocks the simulation thread; the command reader; the fixture recorder that stores the wire bytes; the replayer that writes them back at speed times 50 frames per second; the event writer.</div></details></td><td>new</td><td>crates/stream</td><td>+1320</td></tr>
      <tr class="is-modified"><td><details><summary><span class="pl-chip">domain</span> crates/engine/src/sim.rs, record.rs, validate.rs, data/tuning.rs</summary><div class="pl-card">take_events() for the three events the engine can produce; a restart flag on the frame and a FanoutSink so one run can write a file and stream at once; the validator reads the flag and the sdlc-debt marker at validate.rs:77 is deleted with its shortcut; a stream block in the tuning schema.</div></details></td><td>modified</td><td>crates/engine/src</td><td>+62 / -11</td></tr>
      <tr class="is-modified"><td><details><summary><span class="pl-chip">data</span> content/tuning.json, content/README.md</summary><div class="pl-card">buffer_ticks 500 and keyframe_interval 50, each with its unit, default, and bound in the modder reference.</div></details></td><td>modified</td><td>content/</td><td>+17</td></tr>
      <tr class="is-modified"><td><details><summary><span class="pl-chip">cli</span> crates/engine-cli/src/cli.rs, main.rs, serve.rs (new), record.rs (new), replay.rs (new), bench.rs</summary><div class="pl-card">Three subcommands: serve runs a match under client control and prints the port, record writes a fixture, replay serves one back. bench gains --stream, which reports ticks delivered per second over the socket and the pause count. Every help line stays at 80 columns or fewer.</div></details></td><td>modified</td><td>crates/engine-cli</td><td>+293 / -4</td></tr>
      <tr class="is-new"><td><details><summary><span class="pl-chip">test</span> crates/stream/tests/hello.rs, throughput.rs, backpressure.rs, fixture.rs, commands.rs, common/mod.rs; crates/protocol/tests/document.rs; crates/engine-cli/tests/cli_args.rs (modified)</summary><div class="pl-card">One integration test per criterion, each binding an ephemeral loopback port in the test process: the hello arrives first; 270,000 ticks arrive in order with the buffer inside its bound; a throttled client pauses and resumes the producer with nothing dropped; a recorded fixture replays byte-identically; an unknown change type is rejected verbatim. The document test parses the message tables and fails if the code and the reference disagree.</div></details></td><td>new</td><td>integration tests</td><td>+830</td></tr>
      <tr class="is-new"><td><details><summary><span class="pl-chip">docs</span> docs/reference/protocol.md, README.md (modified)</summary><div class="pl-card">The protocol reference: the handshake, the version rule, one table per message with every field and type, the tick frame byte layout, the fixture file layout, the command set, and the close codes. The enumeration test reads these tables, so the document cannot drift from the code.</div></details></td><td>new</td><td>docs/reference</td><td>+276</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <div class="pl-risks">
    <aside class="callout callout-error"><div class="callout-hd">R1 · The encoder lands on the hot path</div><div class="callout-body">encode_into writes into one reused buffer owned by the sink; the channel carries a fixed-size frame, never a Vec per tick; step 19 measures bench --stream against the 384 ms and 5.62 MB tripwires before any test is called done.</div></aside>
    <aside class="callout callout-error"><div class="callout-hd">R2 · Determinism through a second sink</div><div class="callout-body">FanoutSink calls A then B in order and returns the first error; the determinism test keeps running on the file path; the fixture test compares recorded bytes rather than re-encoded ones.</div></aside>
    <aside class="callout callout-warn"><div class="callout-hd">R3 · tungstenite buffers without a bound by default</div><div class="callout-body">max_write_buffer_size defaults to usize::MAX, so a slow client would grow the write buffer instead of blocking. Set it to four keyframes and treat WriteBufferFull as the same pause signal as a full channel.</div></aside>
    <aside class="callout callout-warn"><div class="callout-hd">R4 · A delta step wider than one signed byte</div><div class="callout-body">A signed byte covers 1.27 m per tick and the 40 m/s ball cap is 0.80 m, but a restart teleport is unbounded. The encoder forces a keyframe on overflow and the codec test drives a restart.</div></aside>
    <aside class="callout callout-warn"><div class="callout-hd">R5 · The Origin allowlist blocks the real viewer</div><div class="callout-body">Accept any http://localhost or http://127.0.0.1 origin regardless of port, and name the origin in the refusal line so viewer-pitch can diagnose it in one look.</div></aside>
    <aside class="callout callout-warn"><div class="callout-hd">R6 · change.queue_id is outside the contract vocabulary</div><div class="callout-body">Block A reserves change.queued_tick and change.rejected_reason but no key for the identifier an acknowledgement returns. Emitted as an additive extra and settled by the observability audit.</div></aside>
    <aside class="callout callout-info"><div class="callout-hd">R7 · Three threads in one binary</div><div class="callout-body">The channel disconnect is the shutdown signal in both directions; every test joins its handles with a timeout and the command-line test asserts the process exits.</div></aside>
    <aside class="callout callout-info"><div class="callout-hd">R8 · A stale port file after a crash</div><div class="callout-body">serve removes engine.port on a clean exit, and the hello message carries match.id, so a page that reaches the wrong engine can tell and reconnect.</div></aside>
  </div>
